{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:arbitrarily-large-prime-power-lcm","Arbitrarily large prime-power LCM\njumps","<p><em> For every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> there is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. One may take <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> for any prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>. The positions <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> are also strict LCM jumps for\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span> is the next required power of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span>. The restriction excludes <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The second condition in the\nnext definition asks for the additional residue margin along this\nexplicit sequence. Knowing these jump positions does not establish that\nmargin.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:sufficient-inequality","A sufficient inequality","<p><em> If,\nfor every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there is\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. This is the sufficient\ncondition defined above, not a claim that the inequality holds at\narbitrarily large indices. Equality at the edge is allowed: the\ncondition is nonnegativity, not strict positivity.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:certificate-four-tail-combination","","<p><em> Use <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> from Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:e2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e2\">6.60</a>. For all <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The bound\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> follows by\ngrouping the nonnegative remainders as in that theorem. Compared with\nthe <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> bound obtained\nby treating the two diagonal differences separately, it saves <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></math></span>. This is a strict improvement when\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>; optimality for totient\ntails is not asserted.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:one-explicit-witness","One explicit witness","<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></math></span>, direct integer evaluation gives\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>149906</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><mn>32768</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18834</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>282.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>282</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>18834</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>32486</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. This is the one\ninstance singled out in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:e2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e2\">6.60</a>, not a claim of\nuniqueness or of the smallest possible height. The implication from a\nquantified family is Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:e2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e2\">6.60</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:denominators-mobius-sums","","<p><em> For every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mtext> prime</mtext><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> as above, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Moreover, some <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span> satisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed,\nBertrand’s postulate makes <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span> nonempty. Its members satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></math></span>, giving the size bounds. The\ndivisibility follows from Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:b6\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b6\">6.6</a>; divisibility of a\npositive reduced denominator also gives the product inequality.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:additional-approximation-hypothesis","The additional approximation\nhypothesis","<p><em> A sequence of rationals <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span> proves irrationality of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> if </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>S</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>for all sufficiently large </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟶</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is\nthe rational-separation criterion of Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:d1\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d1\">6.22</a>: if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> were reduced, every unequal <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span> would instead satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>. An\nupper bound tending to zero for this product is needed. A lower bound\nfor the denominator cannot establish it; improving a lower bound does\nnot change the actual approximation error.</em></p>\n<p><em>A different argument could deduce a residue certificate from the\nsurviving divisor and further information about the complementary term.\nThe denominator theorem alone contains no such separation statement.\nNeither sufficient argument is established by its lower bound, and these\nare not claimed to exhaust possible approaches.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:rationality-carry-rank","","<p><em> For every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the retained dyadic totient\nsections form a basis for all sections through level <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span>, of dimension <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The proof uses the Chinese\nremainder theorem and Dirichlet’s theorem. The complete level-zero\ntruncation, consisting only of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi></math></span>, has dimension one.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:rationality-carry-rank-2","","<p><em> If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, choose an integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. For every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>dim</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">↦</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe proof is given in Section <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#sec:mahler-defect\">10.8</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:additional-hypothesis-totient-specific-rank","An additional hypothesis for a\ntotient-specific rank argument","<p><em> A rank bound that contradicts the\nlower bound <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> would have to use\nadditional arithmetic of the actual totient coefficients. The generic\nproposal that rationality bounds the rank of every integral carry\nsatisfying <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> is\ndisproved by the comparison sequence recorded in the short paper: its\ncarry rank is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> at\nevery level. It is therefore not a remaining general lemma from which\ntotient irrationality follows. The conditional Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:period-not-rank\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:period-not-rank\">1.7</a> records\neventual periodicity modulo <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span>\ntogether with unbounded rational rank. Periodicity of the residue\nsequences concerns values in a finite quotient; it does not bound the\nrational span of the integer-valued sections. The <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> comparison shows that this\ndistinction persists for a rational coefficient series.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:rational-series-preserving-totient-parity","A rational series preserving\ntotient parity and the stated separation properties","<p><em> There exists\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> such that:\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span> for all <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>; <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span>; <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for every\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>; for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> there are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi></math></span> pairs of coefficients <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> starting beyond <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span>, with starting positions separated by\nmore than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span>; and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#lem:complement-divisibility-after-multiplication","Complement divisibility after\nmultiplication","<p><em> Write <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>\nin lowest terms, where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi></math></span> be a positive divisor of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi></math></span>. If the reduced denominator of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi></math></span> divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#lem:nonvanishing-unique-largest-denominator-exponent","Nonvanishing from a unique largest\ndenominator exponent","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>I</mi></math></span>\nbe a finite set, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math></span>, and suppose that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math></span> satisfies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></math></span>. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math></span> is odd, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In particular,\nthe sum is nonzero.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:nonzero-minor-survives-inverse-phase","A nonzero minor survives\ninverse-phase column weights","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be an integer, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> be nonnegative\nintegers, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> be nonzero complex\nnumbers. Write <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></math></span>,\nand suppose <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> for some\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>. Multiplying column <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></math></span> gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThus a nonzero determinant remains nonzero while row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> becomes constant. If all <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, its absolute value is also\nunchanged.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:tail-integrality-lcm-grid","Tail integrality on an LCM grid","<p><em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:finite-grid-certificate-gives-nonintegral","A finite-grid certificate gives a\nnonintegral pair","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> be finite and\nnonempty. For each <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span>, put\n<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></munderover><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Suppose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span> and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> for some\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span>. The proof and a\nfour-point example are given in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b10a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b10a\">6.56</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:exact-dyadic-rank-infinite-dimensionality","Exact dyadic rank and\ninfinite-dimensionality","<p><em> For every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the family </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">↦</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">↦</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>∪</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">↦</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>r</mi><mtext> odd</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> is linearly independent over <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. It contains <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> sequences and spans the sections\nthrough level <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span> by the zero- and\neven-residue reductions.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=e6abdbcd8877"}
